/*
RMSTEST_28a - can a single land with clumping_factor -5 fragment
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 13, and item 3's residual

QUESTION
RMSTEST_21 produced a land at clumping_factor -5 that came back in SIX
disconnected pieces, and one at cf 0 in three. That should be impossible.
Sec.6.1 grows a land by sampling tiles 4-adjacent to already-owned tiles, and a
region grown that way is 4-connected by construction. So either something else
is editing land terrain after growth, or NEGATIVE CLUMPING IS NOT FRONTIER
EXPANSION AT ALL - which would be a finding about Sec.6.1's growth model rather
than about its clumping table.

That is why this is not a table question and does not belong with items 2 and 3.

WHAT RMSTEST_21 ALREADY RULED OUT
  Cliffs           S3 post-dates land growth, and cliffs replaced no terrain
  Neighbour lands  origin stamps precede growth, and growth rejects owned tiles

WHAT THIS SCRIPT RULES OUT, that RMSTEST_21 could not
RMSTEST_21 ran six lands at once. This runs ONE, on an otherwise empty map, so
no second land can be implicated even in principle. Two further variables are
pinned rather than left at their defaults:

  border_fuzziness 0   Fuzziness converts tiles at the land boundary
                       (RMSTEST_23 measured penetration at 1 tile). A boundary
                       process that removes tiles is an obvious candidate for
                       detaching a one-tile-wide neck, and leaving it at its
                       default would have left that candidate live.
  land_position 50 50  Origin pinned at map center so the land cannot run into
                       a map edge and appear to split around it.

NO <CLIFF_GENERATION> SECTION, deliberately. An EMPTY cliff header still
generates cliffs - that is what contaminated the first calibration batch on
2026-08-01. Do not add one to be safe.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200). Bigger than the land needs, so growth never reaches a
map edge. Player count is irrelevant; use any.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. EXACTLY ONE SNOW patch. If Sec.6.1's growth model is right this is not a
   close call, it is a structural guarantee, and any other answer refutes the
   model rather than adjusting a constant.
2. If it fragments, the SIZE DISTRIBUTION names the mechanism, and this is the
   real payload of the run:
     one large patch plus several single tiles
         -> a boundary process is shedding tiles. Since fuzziness is off here,
            that would point at something not currently modelled at all.
     several patches of comparable size
         -> not frontier expansion. Negative clumping is scattering seeds and
            growing each, which is a different algorithm, and Sec.6.1's growth
            model needs rewriting rather than retuning.
     one large patch plus a few small clusters
         -> frontier expansion that can jump a gap, i.e. the candidate set is
            not strictly 4-adjacent
3. Total SNOW area near 400, allowing the ~3 percent growth overshoot from item
   7d. A total far below 400 means growth is terminating early, which would be
   a third and separate finding.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW

RUN IT THREE TIMES. Fragmentation may be probabilistic, and one connected
result does not settle it.

MATCHED PAIR: run RMSTEST_28b (cf 0) in the same sitting. RMSTEST_21 saw both
fragment, and whether the mechanism is the same at 0 as at -5 tells us whether
this is about negative values specifically or about low ones generally.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor -5
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 50
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
